/*
Problem: Construct Binary Search Tree from Preorder Traversal (LC 1008)
Topic: Binary Search Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

In preorder traversal:

Root → Left → Right

The first element is always the root.

Every value smaller than the root
belongs to the left subtree.

Every value greater than the root
belongs to the right subtree.

Instead of searching for the split
point every time, keep an index and
an upper bound.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Maintain an index 'i' pointing to
   the current preorder element.

2. If:

   • i reaches the end, or

   • preorder[i] > bound,

   return NULL.

3. Create the current node using
   preorder[i].

4. Increment i.

5. Build:

   • Left subtree with bound =
     root->val.

   • Right subtree with the same
     bound received from the parent.

6. Return the root.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Preorder:

[8,5,1,7,10,12]

Tree:

        8
      /   \
     5     10
    / \      \
   1   7      12

--------------------------------------------------

Dry Run:

helper(bound = ∞)

Create 8

Left:

helper(bound = 8)

Create 5

Left:

helper(bound = 5)

Create 1

Next value = 7

7 > 5

Return NULL

Right:

7 > 5

Return NULL

Return node 1

Back to 5

Right:

helper(bound = 8)

Create 7

Next value = 10

10 > 7

Return NULL

Return node 7

Back to 8

Right:

helper(bound = ∞)

Create 10

Right:

Create 12

Done.

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

The preorder sequence is consumed only
once.

The 'bound' ensures that nodes never
cross into another subtree.

Every element is processed exactly
once.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Each node is visited once.

Space : O(h)

Recursion stack.

Worst case:

O(n)

Balanced BST:

O(log n)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Binary Search Tree
Recursion
Preorder Traversal
*/
